  "nameStory": "The Parnassia Miner gets its name from its close relationship with a delicate white wildflower called Parnassia, also known as grass-of-Parnassus. This specialized little bee has evolved to be a mining expert, creating underground tunnels where it raises its young, just like a tiny subterranean miner. The scientific name honors both its mining lifestyle and its floral partnership with Parnassia flowers.",
  "description": "The Parnassia Miner is a small, solitary bee that's about the size of a honeybee but with a more compact, robust build. These bees are typically dark brown to black with subtle golden or reddish hairs covering their fuzzy bodies. Females are slightly larger than males, reaching about half an inch long, and they're excellent diggers who create individual nest burrows in the ground. Unlike social bees that live in hives, each female Parnassia Miner works alone to excavate her own underground nursery chambers.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Body Color and Hair",
      "description": "Dark brown to blackish body covered in short, dense hairs that may appear golden or reddish in sunlight. The hair feels soft and fuzzy if you could touch it, giving the bee a slightly 'furry' appearance.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and Build",
      "description": "Compact and robust, about half an inch long with a sturdy, barrel-shaped abdomen. Noticeably more solid-looking than the slender build of sweat bees.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight Pattern",
      "description": "Flies with a steady, purposeful buzzing sound, often hovering briefly over flowers before landing. Less erratic than many other small bees.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leg Color",
      "description": "Legs are typically dark with dense hair for collecting pollen. When carrying pollen, the leg hairs appear dusty white or pale yellow.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Head Shape",
      "description": "Relatively large head compared to body size, with prominent dark eyes. The face appears somewhat flattened when viewed head-on.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Parnassia Miners in wet meadows, stream edges, and boggy areas where their host plant Parnassia grows throughout New England. They prefer areas with well-drained but moisture-retentive soils for digging their nest burrows, often on gentle slopes or raised areas near wetlands. You'll typically find them at elevations from sea level up to about 2,000 feet in mountainous regions.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults are not yet active. Developing larvae remain in underground cells from the previous year's nesting.",
    "summer": "Peak activity from July through early September when Parnassia flowers bloom. Look for females digging small burrows in soil and visiting the white, five-petaled Parnassia flowers.",
    "fall": "Activity continues into early fall as long as Parnassia flowers are blooming. Late-season individuals are often females completing their final nesting activities before winter.",
    "winter": "Adults have died off. Next generation overwinters as larvae in underground cells, developing slowly until the following summer."
  },
  "funFact": "The Parnassia Miner is so specialized that it has co-evolved with its host plant to the point where the bee's entire life cycle is timed perfectly with Parnassia's blooming period - if climate change shifts the flower's timing by even a few weeks, entire local populations of these bees could disappear!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other small, dark mining bees in the same genus, but the Parnassia Miner's association with wet meadows and Parnassia flowers is a dead giveaway. Remember: 'Parnassia plus wet places equals Parnassia Miner' - other similar mining bees typically prefer drier habitats and different flowers.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called a 'miner,' this bee doesn't actually mine anything underground - the name refers to their habit of excavating tunnel-like nests in soil. The term 'mining bee' is used for the entire Andrena genus, which includes over 60 species in New England alone, making this one of our most diverse groups of native bees.",
  "compareHints": "Among the many Andrena species in New England, the Parnassia Miner stands out for its wet habitat preferences and late summer flight period. While most mining bees are spring or early summer fliers that visit common flowers like dandelions or fruit blossoms, the Parnassia Miner is a late-season specialist of an uncommon wetland wildflower. Its robust build also distinguishes it from more delicate species like the Spring Beauty Miner or slender species like the Violet Miner.",
